The issue is to be blamed on the SymbiDisease or the classifiers overfitting, this is to be discovered.


The SymbiPredict dataset has 4961 rows where 4657 are duplicates and only 304 are unique combinations. This means that the duplicate rows
of the testing dataset are causing overfitting and same diagnosis made by all machines.

Therefore, this is not a bug to be debugged but a synthetic dataset issue.

Due to the dataset not being evaluating enough, I will have to manually flip the 0 into 1 and 1 into 0 to impersonate the testing dataset
as a real-world noisy data. In this way, the machines would finally be evaluated on unseen data and the one which deserves to be deployed
will be clearly identified.


def corrupt(df, flip_frac=0.15, seed=42):
    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)
    mask = rng.random(df.shape) < flip_frac
    return df ^ mask.astype(int)

corrupt() takes the dataframe to be corrupted which is df = wow(the testing symptoms dataframe). Only 15% of the cells will have their 
values flipped. Random seed is to be fixed for reproducibility which means every machine should get the same corrupted testing
symptoms for every corrupt() call. ALL cells of the original symptoms dataframe will be assigned a decimal between 0 and 1. 
The cells with decimal below 0.15 will be assigned True whereas the rest of them will be assigned False. This is the hard setting of
boundary to ensure that only 15% of the dataframe will have its cell values flipped. Then the True will be masked to 1 and False into 0.
The cell with 1 will have their original flipped and the cell with 0 will get to keep their original value as it is.

This is how the corruption of testing symptoms take place.

